<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04GE">Remark <span data-tag="04GE">3.2.2.17</span> <span class="named">(Homotopy Invariance)</span>.</a> In the situation of Remark <a data-tag="00VZ" href="/tag/00VZ">3.2.2.16</a>, suppose that $f: X \rightarrow Y$ is a homotopy equivalence. It follows from Proposition <a data-tag="00WW" href="/tag/00WW">3.2.1.13</a> that the homotopy class $[f]$ determines an isomorphism from $(X,x)$ to $(Y,y)$ in the pointed homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}_{\ast }$. In particular, the induced map $\pi _{n}(X,x) \rightarrow \pi _{n}(Y,y)$ is an isomorphism of groups for all $n &gt; 0$ (and a bijection of sets for $n = 0$). </p>
